ButterEye: keep the GPU instance and the loaded model across mpv's seeks.

mpv rebuilds the whole VapourSynth core on every seek, and LoadPlugin loads
this module again with it. Upstream counted its filters and called
ncnn::destroy_gpu_instance() when the last one was freed (and on six error
paths), so every seek paid for a fresh Vulkan instance and a fresh model load.
Measured on the dev box (RTX 4090, 640x360, rife-v4.26, gpu_thread=2): the
re-created core took 1.0-1.3 s to build RIFE() and 0.2-0.3 s more to free it;
mpv logged 1.1-1.4 s between "initializing" and "initialized" after each seek,
and seek -> playback-restart took 1.5-1.9 s.

- The instance is created once and lives as long as the process. ncnn's own
  atexit hook (gpu.cpp) still destroys it at exit, as before.
- The module pins itself (dladdr + dlopen(RTLD_NOLOAD | RTLD_NODELETE); on
  Windows GetModuleHandleEx(..._PIN)) so freeing the core cannot unload it and
  take the instance and the cached model with it.
- The RIFE object of the last filter is kept, keyed by everything its
  constructor and load() take (gpu, tta, uhd, v2/v4, padding, model folder), and
  shared with the next filter that asks for the same. Filters already run
  several process() calls at once on one object (gpu_thread), so sharing it
  adds no new concurrency.
- When no filter is left, the frame buffers are given back: the host pool and
  the device's pooled blob/staging allocators that process() borrows (these
  would otherwise keep 0.45 GiB at 720p, 0.9 GiB at 1080p, 3.7 GiB at 2160p for
  as long as mpv runs, e.g. after interpolation is turned off or another engine
  is picked). The idle cost is then the instance plus the model's weights,
  about 116 MiB for rife-v4.26 whatever the resolution. Re-allocating them costs
  the next seek about 0.1 s at 1080p.
- A filter is counted from the moment it creates (or reuses) the instance, and
  every early return of rifeCreate gives the count back, so the instance can
  never be dropped under a filter that is still loading its model.
- A failed GPU submit (vkQueueSubmit / vkWaitForFences, as after a lost device)
  is remembered; a model that saw one is no longer handed to new filters, and
  once the last filter is gone the cached model and the instance are destroyed,
  so the next filter starts on a fresh instance. A failed create_gpu_instance()
  is likewise forgotten when no filter is alive. The old counter also went
  negative when the format check threw before the instance was created; that
  is gone with it.

Windows: the instance is now always destroyed by ncnn's atexit hook, which in
a DLL runs during process detach, possibly after the Vulkan driver's DLLs have
been detached. Only Linux has been tested; check that mpv quits cleanly on real
Windows hardware before the Windows build applies this patch.

After (640x360): RIFE() in a re-created core 0 ms (another model: 0.27-0.37 s);
mpv's re-init 5-15 ms and seek -> playback-restart 0.25-0.36 s; at 1080p with
--hr-seek=no 0.38-0.40 s (was 1.8 s). Output is bit-identical.

